The lists are scanned into slices first, then the slices are added one position at a time. A missing position is treated as zero, just like a shorter number in normal addition.

The answer is built after all digits have been calculated, so neither input list is changed. This is straightforward, but it uses more temporary memory than the direct pointer method.

```go
func addTwoNumbers(linkedList1 *ListNode, linkedList2 *ListNode) *ListNode {
    firstDigits := make([]int, 0)
    secondDigits := make([]int, 0)

    for current := linkedList1; current != nil; current = current.next {
        firstDigits = append(firstDigits, current.data)
    }
    for current := linkedList2; current != nil; current = current.next {
        secondDigits = append(secondDigits, current.data)
    }

    resultDigits := make([]int, 0)
    carry := 0
    longest := len(firstDigits)
    if len(secondDigits) > longest {
        longest = len(secondDigits)
    }

    for index := 0; index < longest; index++ {
        firstDigit := 0
        if index < len(firstDigits) {
            firstDigit = firstDigits[index]
        }

        secondDigit := 0
        if index < len(secondDigits) {
            secondDigit = secondDigits[index]
        }

        total := firstDigit + secondDigit + carry
        resultDigits = append(resultDigits, total%10)
        carry = total / 10
    }

    if carry != 0 {
        resultDigits = append(resultDigits, carry)
    }

    dummy := &ListNode{}
    tail := dummy
    for _, digit := range resultDigits {
        tail.next = &ListNode{data: digit}
        tail = tail.next
    }

    return dummy.next
}
```

The final carry is added after the main loop, which handles cases such as `9 -> 9` plus `1` correctly. Empty lists also work because their slices stay empty.

Time complexity: `O(m + n)`.
Space complexity: `O(m + n)`, including the temporary slices and result list.

The lists keep the least significant digit at the head, so the addition can be done from left to right. A dummy node makes it easy to attach every new result digit.

If one list ends earlier, its digit is treated as 0. The loop also checks `carry`, because inputs such as `9 -> 9` and `1` need one extra result node.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* addTwoNumbers(ListNode* &linkedList1, ListNode* &linkedList2) {
        ListNode dummy(0);
        ListNode* tail = &dummy;
        int carry = 0;

        while (linkedList1 != nullptr || linkedList2 != nullptr || carry != 0) {
            int firstDigit = linkedList1 != nullptr ? linkedList1->data : 0;
            int secondDigit = linkedList2 != nullptr ? linkedList2->data : 0;
            int total = firstDigit + secondDigit + carry;

            carry = total / 10;
            tail->next = new ListNode(total % 10);
            tail = tail->next;

            if (linkedList1 != nullptr) {
                linkedList1 = linkedList1->next;
            }
            if (linkedList2 != nullptr) {
                linkedList2 = linkedList2->next;
            }
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
};
```

For `2 -> 4 -> 3` and `5 -> 6 -> 4`, the totals are 7, 10, and 8, giving `7 -> 0 -> 8`.

Time complexity: `O(max(m, n))`.
Space complexity: `O(max(m, n))` for the returned list, with `O(1)` extra working space.

Instead of allocating a result node for every digit, this version writes the answer into the longer input list. The shorter list is read normally, and any remaining carry is applied to the rest of the longer list.

The input list is changed. If the second list is longer, the method swaps the references first so the first list has enough nodes for the main result.

```java
class Solution {
    public ListNode addTwoNumbers(ListNode l1, ListNode l2) {
        int firstLength = length(l1);
        int secondLength = length(l2);

        if (secondLength > firstLength) {
            return addTwoNumbers(l2, l1);
        }

        ListNode resultHead = l1;
        ListNode previous = null;
        int carry = 0;

        while (l1 != null && l2 != null) {
            int total = l1.data + l2.data + carry;
            l1.data = total % 10;
            carry = total / 10;

            previous = l1;
            l1 = l1.next;
            l2 = l2.next;
        }

        while (l1 != null) {
            int total = l1.data + carry;
            l1.data = total % 10;
            carry = total / 10;

            previous = l1;
            l1 = l1.next;
        }

        if (carry != 0) {
            previous.next = new ListNode(carry);
        }

        return resultHead;
    }

    private int length(ListNode node) {
        int count = 0;
        while (node != null) {
            count++;
            node = node.next;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

Example: `9 -> 9` plus `1` changes the first list to `0 -> 0 -> 1`. The last node is created only when the final carry needs extra space.

Time complexity: `O(m + n)` because the lengths and digits are scanned.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, apart from a possible final node. The original longer list is modified.

This version first copies the digits into arrays. Since the head already represents the units digit, the arrays can be processed from index 0 without reversing them. The result digits are then used to create a new linked list.

It is a little less space friendly than using only pointers, but the separate arrays make the digit positions easy to inspect.

```javascript
class Solution {
    addTwoNumbers(linkedList1, linkedList2) {
        const firstDigits = [];
        const secondDigits = [];

        let current = linkedList1;
        while (current !== null) {
            firstDigits.push(current.data);
            current = current.next;
        }

        current = linkedList2;
        while (current !== null) {
            secondDigits.push(current.data);
            current = current.next;
        }

        const resultDigits = [];
        let carry = 0;
        const length = Math.max(firstDigits.length, secondDigits.length);

        for (let index = 0; index < length; index++) {
            const firstDigit = index < firstDigits.length ? firstDigits[index] : 0;
            const secondDigit = index < secondDigits.length ? secondDigits[index] : 0;
            const total = firstDigit + secondDigit + carry;

            resultDigits.push(total % 10);
            carry = Math.floor(total / 10);
        }

        if (carry !== 0) {
            resultDigits.push(carry);
        }

        const dummy = new ListNode(0);
        let tail = dummy;

        for (const digit of resultDigits) {
            tail.next = new ListNode(digit);
            tail = tail.next;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

A quick check with `[2, 4, 3]` and `[5, 6]` gives totals 7, 10, and 3, so the returned digits are `[7, 0, 4]`.

Time complexity: `O(m + n)`.
Space complexity: `O(m + n)` for the digit arrays and the returned list.

